    "result_text": "Green. The full suite passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nMoved date-derived values onto a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starting **1 July**), keeping structural impact low by adding one small, cohesive helper rather than growing any existing class or method.\n\n**New unit** \u2014 `FiscalYear.java` (function/owner package): a tiny helper with `of(LocalDate)` (July\u2013December \u2192 next calendar year's FY) and `label(Owner)` (`FY<YY>` from the stored, already business-day-adjusted registration date, null-safe). It has near-zero surrounding complexity, so every call site stays cheap.\n\n**Minimal edits to existing code:**\n- `MembershipNumber.of` \u2014 one line: year segment now `FiscalYear.of(registrationDate) % 100` instead of the raw calendar year.\n- `MembershipLevel.hasTenure` \u2014 one expression: tenure is now `FiscalYear.of(now) \u2212 FiscalYear.of(registration) >= 1` (elapsed fiscal years) instead of a 365-day window; dropped the now-unused constant and `ChronoUnit` import.\n- `OwnerMapper` \u2014 one `@Mapping` line exposing `fiscalYear` via `FiscalYear.label(owner)`, consistent with the existing expression mappings.\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added the read-only `fiscalYear` property to the `Owner` schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field) and corrected the `membershipNumber`/`membershipPoints` descriptions.\n\nThe business-day roll (`BuildOwner` \u2192 `BusinessDay.rollForward`) already stores the adjusted date on the owner, so all three fiscal-year consumers derive from it automatically.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreReturnsFiscalYear` (asserting `fiscalYear` matches `FY\\d{2}`) and the whole suite pass \u2014 the audit log confirms a September registration correctly yields `M27` / `FY27`.",
